cartridge "MINESWEEPER/B.CAMPBELL/1989"

' MINESWEEPER
' A ColecoVision game based on "Minesweeper" by Ben Campbell, COMPUTE!'s
' Gazette July 1989 (Commodore 64 machine language started by the one-line
' loader 10 SYS (2068); the program's own title screen reads "** MINE
' SWEEPER ** BY BEN CAMPBELL"). Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made
' by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by Amy Bienvenu.
'
' An arcade game, not the logic puzzle of the same name: a minesweeper
' ship hovers over an industrial landscape that scrolls under it, turns
' left or right and fires along its line; floating mines come in from both
' edges. Survive long enough and the sector's mine layer comes out at the
' right edge; destroy it and the sector is cleared. Five sectors.
'
' From the original (orig/minesweeper.prg, read in orig/dis.txt, run in
' jsc64):
' - The ship stays at one x and moves up and down; the stick left/right
'   turns it (a short turning animation) and the ground scrolls the way it
'   faces. The ground is a 256-column, four-row landscape (here the
'   original's own map, $1669-$19FF); touching it destroys the ship.
' - Fire (held fires again) sends one shot at a time along the ship's
'   line, 16 pixels a frame.
' - Three mines, each one at a time, come in from the left or right edge
'   at random (SID noise): the seeker (2 pixels a frame, drifting a pixel
'   a frame toward the ship's height), the dart (4 a frame, at the ship's
'   height when it starts) and the drifter (1 a frame, riding the wave
'   table $1A66). A shot mine is worth 1 point; touching one destroys the
'   ship.
' - The sector clock is the C64 jiffy clock's middle byte: after 4 x 256
'   jiffies (1024 frames) no new mines come; when the last has gone the
'   mine layer (two sprites) rises and falls at the right edge 2 pixels a
'   frame and fires bolts along its height 6 a frame, one at a time; the
'   bolts cannot be shot. Destroying it is worth 15, counted up; then
'   NICE BLASTING - SECTOR CLEARED! and the next sector in new colours.
' - A lost ship keeps the clock's 256-frame steps (the jiffy low byte is
'   reset), the field is cleared and GET READY... shown.
' - Nine ships; one more each time the score reaches a multiple of 50.
' - After the fifth sector: GREAT GOING!!! ALL SECTORS CLEARED!!! 100
'   POINTS BONUS... GAME COMPLETED!!!
'
' Liberties: speeds for a human player: the C64's main loop ran about 280
' times a second, so the ship moved about 560 pixels a second and the
' ground scrolled 280 characters a second; here the ship moves 2 pixels a
' frame and the ground a character every 3 frames. The interrupt-driven
' speeds (shot, mines, layer, bolt) are the C64's per frame. A mine slot
' waits CoolF frames after its mine is gone before a new one may come (the
' C64 refilled it within a few milliseconds), so a player has time to see
' it. The dart starts 2 pixels above the ship's height (the C64's 10, at
' its own sprite offsets). The C64 counted the layer's sprite collisions
' once a main-loop pass, 40 to destroy it (about 5-8 shots); here 6 shots,
' shown in a gauge. Hits are box tests on the drawn shapes; an exploding
' mine is harmless; the shot is tested along its whole 16-pixel step.
' The side a mine comes from is a fair coin (the C64 reused the last noise
' byte). The field is 28 columns wide (the C64's 31), score, ships and
' sector shown above the field (the C64 had a side panel). At the end:
' the message, then the title (the C64 looped its finale forever).
' Additions: a title screen with the original title, credits, a title
' tune, a turning frame for the ship, explosion frames.
'
' Standing still cannot last: the seeker closes on the ship's height and
' the dart is aimed at it, from both sides, so the ship has to turn, fire
' and dodge. Holding fire one way leaves the other side open.
'
' Controls: controller 1 (or 2): up/down fly, left/right turn, either
' button fires. On the title, up and down choose, a button chooses.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off,
' display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after; the title
' tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

